PENDING CI HANDOFF — apply this, then delete this file.

Why this file exists
--------------------
Sixteen repository-level gates carry a written reason for not running in CI.
That reason used to point at `/tmp/b806-ci-gates.patch`, which no longer exists:
the handoff was stored in a directory the operating system is free to empty, and
it was emptied. Batch 899 measured that failure rather than assuming it, and
Batch 900 moved the handoff here, where the repository keeps it until a human
applies it.

What is in it, and what is deliberately not
------------------------------------------
Only the `.github/workflows/ci.yml` steps. The other half of the change — the
sixteen `noCiReason` lines that go stale the moment CI reaches those gates — is
**not** in here on purpose, because the repository already knows how to find
them. `scripts/verify-gate-wiring.mjs` reports each one as
`[stale-ci-reason]`, naming the gate, precisely so that hand-maintained list
does not have to exist.

How to apply
------------
    git apply .github/pending/ci-repo-gates.patch
    ./scripts/verify-gate-wiring.mjs          # names the stale exemptions
    # delete the noCiReason line for each gate it lists, in scripts/gate-registry.mjs
    rm .github/pending/ci-repo-gates.patch    # this file has served its purpose

After the `noCiReason` lines are gone, one standing gap remains by decision
rather than by permission: `scripts/verify-webui-e2e-mock.sh`, whose cost is
recorded in `E2E_MOCK_NOT_IN_CI`.

Why not just push this
----------------------
Editing `.github/workflows/` needs a token with the `workflow` scope. Batch 899
pushed it and the remote answered, verbatim:

    refusing to allow an OAuth App to create or update workflow
    .github/workflows/ci.yml without workflow scope

`gh` is installed here (2.78.0, at /usr/local/bin/gh) and simply not logged in,
so a human with credentials can do this in one command — the older claim that
the CLI was unavailable was false, and has been removed from the registry.

